ed. Markets price her odds above ninety percent, consistent with raters calling the race Lean or Likely Democratic.", "watch": "A late resurgence of Republican advertising or a national environment breaking toward James could compress the lead in the final week.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Gretchen Whitmer (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Michigan's governorship is open as Gretchen Whitmer is term-limited, and Democrat Jocelyn Benson faces Republican John James. Polling since late September consistently shows Benson ahead, with margins ranging from one to seventeen points and an average near seven. Aggregators in early October placed her lead between seven and eight points. Prediction markets give her roughly a 90 to 95 percent chance of winning, and most race raters call the contest Lean or Likely Democratic.\n\nBenson has raised about $12.5 million to James's $6.4 million and holds a wide cash-on-hand advantage, while recent reporting notes pro-James television spending has dropped, leaving an ad gap. James has sought distance from former President Trump in debates and on tariff policy, and Barack Obama has endorsed Benson. The main question appears to be the final margin rather than the winner.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9339, "margin": 7.39}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9166, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9224, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9193, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8901, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9053, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.911, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.9221, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9131, "margin": 6.8}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.9322, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.912, "margin": 6.8}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.9194, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.9194, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "20